76
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:49,500
So in this podcast we've got scout master ship

77
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:51,500
in seven minutes or less.

78
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:53,500
It's going to be a briefer podcast. this week

79
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:56,500
We'll be recording a scout master panel discussion

80
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:00,500
for podcast 140, which will be coming up next week.

81
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:03,500
But one of the posts on the blog got a lot of attention this week

82
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:05,500
and I thought it would be a good thing to talk about.

83
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:07,500
So let's get started, shall we

84
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:12,500
The scout master ship in seven minutes or less?

85
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:24,500
Now I know most of you follow Andy over at the US

86
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:26,500
Scouting Service Project.

87
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:30,500
Andy is a longtime commissioner and has had an online column

88
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:33,500
where he answers questions that people send in about scouting

89
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:35,500
and it's really great.

90
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:38,500
He publishes a new column every week or two.

91
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:40,500
There was one email that he got and responded to.

92
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:43,500
that I thought was just really interesting.

93
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:45,500
And the email he got was from a parent

94
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:48,500
who had attended summer camp with their son's troop this year

95
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:51,500
and watched as the scout master kind of sat there

96
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:55,500
and watched the scouts try things and fail

97
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:58,500
and the parent was a little incensed about this

98
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:02,500
because it took the scouts a long time to get it.

99
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:04,500
You know the particular activities they were doing

100
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:06,500
and things like that.

101
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:09,500
And of course Andy responded by saying:

102
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:11,500
well, it sounds like a pretty savvy scout master to me.

103
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:13,500
Let the boys learn things.

104
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:15,500
He didn't interfere with them.

105
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:18,500
He kept an eye on things and made sure that you know

106
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:20,500
they weren't doing anything that was dangerous

107
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:23,500
or inappropriate. but he probably did the right thing

108
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:25,500
by not intervening.

109
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:28,500
Well, I posted the email and the response

110
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:31,500
at scoutmastercgcom and it's under the title:

111
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:34,500
are you a safety net or a nursemaid?

112
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:37,500
And I drew that right from Andy's response.

113
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:40,500
He said this scout master was acting more like a safety net

114
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:42,500
instead of a nursemaid.

115
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:45,500
There's been a really great active discussion.

116
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:47,500
Some of the principles involved in that

117
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:50,500
in the comment section on that post,

118
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:52,500
And you know what

119
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:54,500
I mean. I really appreciate people coming over

120
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:56,500
and reading the posts and participating,

121
00:05:56,500 --> 00:05:59,500
but the thing is is that what you really want to read

122
00:05:59,500 --> 00:06:01,500
on most of these posts are the comments,

123
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:03,500
Because that's where things you know

124
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:06,500
we'll present an idea, but that's where things get discussed

125
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:08,500
and you get different points of view

126
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:11,500
and it can be really helpful.

127
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:14,500
So we've gotten several comments on this one this week

128
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:16,500
and we've made some replies

129
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:18,500
and I thought it would be useful to talk about.

130
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:21,500
This is one of these subjects that we return to again and again.

131
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:23,500
What do you do?

132
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:25,500
You know, I mean very broadly,

133
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:27,500
what do you do as a scout leader,

134
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:29,500
as a scout master in particular,

135
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:31,500
but as any volunteer leader?

136
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:34,500
You know we're watching the boys do whatever

137
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:36,500
and kind of muddle through

138
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:38,500
and they're making mistakes

139
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:40,500
and they're getting frustrated

140
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:42,500
and you know they're pushing themselves

141
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:44,500
to the point of failure.

142
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:46,500
and you know what do we do,

143
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:49,500
How much advice and mentoring

144
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:52,500
and instruction is enough

145
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:54,500
and how much is too much

146
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:57,500
and how much is too little.

147
00:06:57,500 --> 00:06:59,500
Well, you know what?

148
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:01,500
There's just no way to tell you,

149
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:03,500
There's no way to tell you

150
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:06,500
one rule for every single instance

151
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:08,500
in the book there, you know.

152
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:11,500
but there are some, just some conceptual things

153
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:13,500
that may be of assistance to you

154
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:15,500
in learning these.

155
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,500
First of all, let me say this:

156
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:19,500
as you're working along here,

157
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:21,500
learning how to be a scout leader

158
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:23,500
and learning how all this works

159
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:25,500
and mirroring the things

160
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:27,500
that you see your scouts doing-

161
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:29,500
and this isn't bad,

162
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:31,500
because this kind of trial and error thing

163
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:33,500
is the way that we advance right

164
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:34,500
in our work

165
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:36,500
and the way that we become more skillful

166
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:38,500
and experienced in it.

167
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:40,500
Just the way that when scouts are building a campfire

168
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:42,500
or setting up a tent or something like that,

169
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:44,500
you know they got to try out a dozen different ways

170
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:46,500
of doing it before they settle on one

171
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:48,500
that really works for them.

172
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:50,500
So how do you know?

173
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:52,500
How do you know if you're doing too much?

174
00:07:52,500 --> 00:07:54,500
How do you know when you've kind of hit the balance there?

175
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:56,500
Well, I've got a little

176
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:59,500
practical exercise for you. okay,

177
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:01,500
Your next camp out, your next troop meeting,

178
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:04,500
Get a chair and really a chair,

179
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,500
and sit in the chair

180
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:08,500
so that you are in a position

181
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:10,500
to observe what's going on.

182
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:12,500
But you're far enough away

183
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:14,500
that the scouts you know.

184
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:16,500
they may be aware that you're looking

185
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:17,500
and they may not,

186
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:19,500
But just observe.

187
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:20,500
Just observe

188
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:22,500
and sit in that chair

189
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:24,500
and time it for 30 minutes

190
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:27,500
or an hour, 45 minutes, something like that-

191
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:29,500
A decent amount of time-

192
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:31,500
And resolve to do this,

193
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:33,500
Resolve not to get out of the chair

194
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:35,500
and resolve not to speak

195
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:38,500
or react to what's going on with the scouts.

196
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:41,500
Now, naturally-

197
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:44,500
and you know I have to mention this caveat,

198
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:46,500
but naturally it's understood-

199
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:48,500
if they're doing something dangerous

200
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:50,500
or inappropriate

201
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:52,500
you get out of the chair and you speak

202
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:54,500
and you make it stop.

203
00:08:54,500 --> 00:08:56,500
So we all understand that. right,

204
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:58,500
Okay, we're going to react

205
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:00,500
if things are dangerous or inappropriate,

206
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:02,500
But sit there and watch and observe.

207
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:04,500
Now, a useful way to think about this

208
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:07,500
may be that you're like a naturalist, okay,

209
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:09,500
And everybody's seen the shows

210
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:11,500
on TV and things like that.

211
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:13,500
You know, the naturalist goes

212
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:15,500
and they find out where the

213
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:17,500
particular animal or phenomena

214
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:20,500
of the study is, and then they set up a blind right

215
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:22,500
And then they sit in the blind

216
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:24,500
and they kind of peek through it

217
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:27,500
and they're watching, so that they don't interrupt

218
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:29,500
the subject that they're observing

219
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:31,500
And they don't break the natural flow

220
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:33,500
of things happening.

221
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:35,500
And this is what I'm suggesting to you

222
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:37,500
Is just sit and watch,

223
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:39,500
Sit and observe

224
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:41,500
And watch what's happening,

225
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:43,500
Listen to what they're saying,

226
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:45,500
Watch how they're acting.

227
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:47,500
They're not having any investment

228
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:49,500
or participation in what's going on at all

229
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:51,500
other than to observe.

230
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:54,500
Now, if you're a fairly active scout leader

231
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:56,500
who's putting their oar in a lot

232
00:09:56,500 --> 00:09:58,500
and you have a lot of opinions about things

233
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:00,500
and you offer a lot of advice

234
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:02,500
and instruction and things,

235
00:10:02,500 --> 00:10:05,500
this 30 minutes or hour-long session

236
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:07,500
where you sit in the chair

237
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:10,500
is going to be very uncomfortable,

238
00:10:10,500 --> 00:10:12,500
Because I know I've tried it

239
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:14,500
and it's very uncomfortable.

240
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:16,500
You're going to squirm around a little bit.

241
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:19,500
You're going to have 20 things running through your mind

242
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:21,500
about why you need to step in

243
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:23,500
and fix whatever is going on.

244
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:26,500
But resist the temptation

245
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:28,500
and just sit and observe.

246
00:10:28,500 --> 00:10:30,500
Nobody's going to die,

247
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:32,500
Nobody's going to quit scouts

248
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:34,500
because you're sitting and observing

249
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:36,500
for this 30 minutes or hour or so

250
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:38,500
And see what you can learn.

251
00:10:38,500 --> 00:10:40,500
I think that in that time

252
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:42,500
you'll learn more than you would

253
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:44,500
in a week of training

254
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:46,500
in a lot of instances.

255
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:48,500
Once you're sitting and observing

256
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:50,500
and you've resolved,

257
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:52,500
I'm not going to get up

258
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:54,500
and I'm not going to react

259
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:56,500
and I'm not going to speak.

260
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:58,500
I'm just going to watch

261
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:00,500
and see what goes on.

262
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:02,500
then note your reactions

263
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:04,500
to the different things that are happening.

264
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:06,500
Scouts say one thing,

265
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:08,500
or they start doing one thing,

266
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:10,500
or they're doing something one way.

267
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:12,500
or I wish they didn't look at it like this,

268
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:14,500
or I wish they didn't say it like this.

269
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:16,500
Don't judge that,

270
00:11:16,500 --> 00:11:18,500
Just get the reaction

271
